{"id":"60b55a20-e332-4965-b228-438d1a5e2fc6","object":"chat.completion","created":1785006639,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        call_id = call['call_id']\\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        parseable = call['parseable']\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        # Normalize by at least one token to avoid division by zero\\n        norm_gain = gain / max(tokens, 1)\\n        accepted = parseable and norm_gain >= threshold\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            'call_id': call_id,\\n            'accepted': accepted,\\n            'normalized_gain': norm_gain\\n        })\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\\n    return {\\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted_call_ids,\\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) that implements API call loss filtering. The case payload has threshold and calls list. Each call has call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable. For each call, compute gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call. Normalized gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call / prediction_tokens? Actually \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token\" suggests divide by number of prediction tokens, but ensure at least one token (i.e., max(prediction_tokens,1)? But prediction_tokens seems to be integer, likely >=1, but could be zero? Not in fixtures, but handle. Use max(prediction_tokens, 1) to avoid division by zero.\n\nThen accepted if parseable is True and normalized_gain >= threshold.\n\nThen diagnostic per call: accepted (bool), call_id (string), normalized_gain (number). Return accepted_call_ids (list of call_ids that are accepted), and diagnostics list in same order as calls. Normalized gain should be a number (float). We need to return JSON-serializable. Use round maybe? The description says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" but not specified precision. In fixtures, they might expect certain rounding? The output schema says type \"number\", so we can output the float as is. But \"rounded\" might imply rounding to some decimal? Not specified. We'll produce the normalized gain value as computed, not rounded. However, to be safe, maybe round to some reasonable digits? But since not specified, keep as float.\n\nAlso, \"normalized_weights, stronger_counterfactual, inclusive_threshold, stable_order\" are noted as hard contracts. \"normalized_weights\" likely means we compute normalized gain using weights (prediction_tokens). \"stronger_counterfactual\" means gain = loss_without - loss_with (since larger loss_without is stronger counterfactual). \"inclusive_threshold\" means accepted if normalized_gain >= threshold (including equality). \"stable_order\" means diagnostics in supplied call order.\n\nThus algorithm: for each call in case[\"calls\"] (list), compute normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / max(prediction_tokens, 1) (or just / prediction_tokens, but ensure non-zero). Then accepted = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold. Append diagnostic dict with accepted, call_id, normalized_gain. If accepted, add call_id to accepted_call_ids list.\n\nReturn {\"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids, \"diagnostics\": diagnostics}.\n\nEdge cases: missing fields? Assume present per fixture. No mutation of case.\n\nNow write code. Use Python standard library.\n\nThe solve function should be defined. We'll use json module to parse? No, case is a dict already.\n\nWe need to output as JSON with implementation key.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2